\section{Make Allocation and Heap Walking Agree}

A heap object needs more than an address. The component that allocates
it and the component that later walks past it must agree on its
extent, alignment, and reference fields. This is an internal ABI:
the frontend can change the meaning of a vector without touching it,
but changing the location of the vector length affects both generated
code and the collector. A useful first design gives each object a
header with a descriptor and uses descriptors to enumerate reference
slots, as in Appel's account of the compiler interface
\cite{appel2004modern}. A slot is a location the collector can read
and, for moving collection, rewrite. It is not merely the pointer
value previously read from that location.

Assume eight-byte words and sixteen-byte object alignment. Use a
descriptor ID in the first word and a type-specific auxiliary word
next. For a reference vector, that word is the element count. For a
pair, it can hold flags without changing the fixed size. A record
with an unboxed counter cannot use a scan policy of ``follow every
nonzero word.'' The integer may coincide with an aligned heap address.
Conversely, forgetting one real reference need not crash during
tracing; it may only become visible after the reclaimed storage
is reused. The following small layout deliberately distinguishes
slot type from the bytes currently stored in it.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lrrl}
\hline
Kind & Header & Extent & Slots after header \\
\hline
Counter & 16 & 32 & none \\
Pair & 16 & 32 & 0, 1 \\
Ref vector, length 3 & 16 & 48 & 0, 1, 2 \\
Byte vector, length 17 & 16 & 48 & none \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

In \Cref{fig:visual-408}, the last word of the reference vector is
padding. It is not a fourth element. Its address matters to the next
allocation, but its bit pattern must have no effect on reachability.
A heap checker can deliberately fill padding with a pattern that
resembles an address and verify that the slot visitor ignores it.

