% Annotated walkthrough
\section{Reserve, Initialize, and Publish}

\begin{verbatim}
extent(kind, count):
    check count is valid for kind
    bytes := checked_add(16, checked_payload(kind, count))
    return checked_align_up(bytes, 16)

reserve_in_buffer(thread, bytes):
    assert thread.top <= thread.limit
    if bytes > thread.limit - thread.top: return Miss
    start := thread.top
    thread.top := start + bytes
    return start

new_ref_vector(thread, count):
    bytes := extent(RefVector, count)
    p := reserve_in_buffer(thread, bytes)
    if p is Miss:
        refill_or_collect(thread, bytes)
        p := reserve_in_buffer(thread, bytes)
        if p is Miss: signal allocation failure
    # No safepoint until header and slots are initialized.
    p.descriptor := RefVector
    p.aux := count
    for i in 0 .. count-1:
        p.slot[i] := null
    return p
\end{verbatim}

This is a fast path for a cooperative stop-the-world runtime. No
collector walks the reserved interval between allocation and
initialization. The subtraction check avoids forming an overflowing
end address; the size calculation independently checks multiplication,
addition, and rounding. Use the same logical extent calculation in
the debug walker, but check the result against the containing block
before trusting a header read from a damaged heap. When collection
can happen in the refill routine, arguments needed after that call
belong in updateable roots. Here the count is an integer. A variant
that fills the vector with an existing object must root that object
before the refill and reload it afterward.

Publication to another mutator is a separate event from making
the object parseable by the collector. An object containing only
null references may be safely scanned before its intended contents
have been installed; application code must not receive it until
its logical initialization is complete. A concurrent heap walker
cannot use the private-buffer argument above as written: it needs a
way to distinguish a reserved range from a committed range, and a
publication protocol for descriptors and fields. Either arrange
such a protocol explicitly or keep that walker out of private
allocation buffers until the owning thread has retired them.

\section{Account for Fast-Path Waste and Slow-Path Work}

Suppose a buffer has 32 bytes left and a vector needs 48. The
allocator cannot leave the old tail looking like an object if the
heap walker enumerates every address up to the region limit.
The runtime could walk only committed prefixes, or install a
recognized filler covering the remainder. The latter representation
must accommodate the smallest possible tail: with sixteen-byte
alignment and a sixteen-byte filler header it can. Neither design
implies that objects of size larger than a standard buffer should
receive oversized private buffers. Allocate large objects via a
separate path, and still charge their full bytes to collection
triggers. An allocation counter that records only buffer refills
can miss an entire class of heap pressure.

Measure requested payload, rounded allocation, abandoned buffer
tails, and slow-path invocations separately. With a million pairs
the layout above allocates 32 MB for 16 MB of payload. Enlarging
alignment to 32 bytes does not change that case, but a vector
of length three then grows from 48 to 64 bytes. A workload report
that counts objects without sorting by layout will hide the effect.
Likewise, a large buffer may save synchronization without reducing
overall collection cost if it raises the amount of memory retained
in private tails across many mostly idle threads.

\section{Keep Representation Choices Local}

A dynamically typed implementation can put immediate values and
references in the same slot using tags. This changes the visitor:
a descriptor identifies the slot as containing a tagged value,
and the visitor tests and decodes before tracing. It restores
the tag when rewriting a moved pointer. With compressed references
the visitor instead needs a heap-base and encoding convention.
Do not spread either convention through marking and sweeping code.
Use a scanner that produces abstract slots and a representation
layer that implements reading and writing them. A fixed descriptor
bitmap still has a role in such a system: it tells the collector
where a word is a tagged value rather than arbitrary bytes.

Build one regression fixture for each allocation entry point.
Allocate a reference vector across a buffer boundary, place a
sole live reference in its last slot, and force collection. In
the next fixture put the same bit pattern in a byte vector and
test that it does not retain the referent. Round-trip changes
through the moving visitor and finally walk to the next object.
Together those checks exercise size, initialization, and pointer
enumeration; a test that merely checks the returned address is
aligned covers none of the agreements on which tracing depends.

The layout contract also applies during transitions. A class
whose fields can change shape at run time cannot first publish a
descriptor for a reference slot and then fill that slot with an
immediate integer using a representation the descriptor does not
permit. For a first implementation keep the scanning descriptor
immutable for the life of an object and allocate fresh storage
for a representation change. Property lookup may still use a
mutable logical shape if the physical slots all have the same
tagged representation. When later optimizing away tags, test a
collection exactly where the optimized path changes shape, not
only after the mutation has completed. The scanner sees the
object at a machine instruction boundary, not at a source-level
property assignment considered as a single operation.
